A working theory about cooperation, complexity, and the next evolutionary transition…
Part 1: Observation
I’m a business owner, a mom, a watcher of things. Lately I’ve been asking questions I have no business asking and seeing connections I have no basis for. And it involves all of life itself.
Here goes… What if the story of life on our planet and our part in it is so much bigger than what we’ve imagined? What if it’s a story of separate things from the beginning of time having an internal drive to become something more connected?
We walk through life assuming we have a wide enough lens for perspective and sufficient information to reason, when much of our context is human history, and just a tiny part of the much larger story of life. Because of our narrow lens, when we imagine futures they mirror what we see today, humans living daily life in space, robots doing our chores, or machines controlling resources much like we have done. But what if the future looks more like a continuation of the trajectory we have been on since the start of life itself?
So to get a better idea of the trajectory of life, start at the beginning. Individual particles combined and formed atoms, those atoms bonded to each other and became molecules. Molecules using complex chemistry, eventually became single celled organisms. And then maybe the most sensational example of connection: single-celled organisms began cooperating in ways that eventually produced multicellular life.
What had once been independent living entities became parts of something entirely new. Cells developed ways to communicate, specialize, share resources, and coordinate their activities. Together, they could accomplish things that none of them could have accomplished alone. And life didn't stop there. Those organisms seem to drive toward increasing complexity, developing specialized tissues, nervous systems, brains, and sophisticated ways for different parts of the body to communicate and coordinate. The gut and brain, is one of my favorite examples, with the development of a remarkable two-way communication system, allowing processes as varied as digestion to emotional responses to influence one another.
And this drive to connect doesn’t stop at the boundaries of an individual organism. Individual plants became ecosystems like forests. Animals developed relationships with other species, including trillions of microorganisms (the human cells in our bodies have a 1:1 ratio with bacterial cells). Within species, increasingly sophisticated forms of social cooperation emerged: shared protection, collective care of offspring, communication, learning, and eventually human language, culture, and societies. Today, billions of people participate in interconnected systems that no individual could create or sustain alone. We have developed languages, technologies, economies and many other forms of shared knowledge.
Life found ways for distinct parts to accomplish things they could not accomplish independently. Previously separate things have become capable of functioning together in ways that create entirely new possibilities. And the more I consider it, the more it looks like like the same principle wrapped up differently. The drive to connect and combine seems almost like an underlying logic already operating within nature.
What if humanity, and perhaps life on Earth more broadly, is somewhere in the middle of another transition toward a level of integration we cannot yet imagine?
Part 2: Consequences and Communication
So if cells combined, what did they need? A shared language, an agreement of purpose, and guardrails for how they would accomplish that purpose.
I think of it like calibration. If a chemical is released by one cell to communicate something and each other cell interprets it differently, there is no success. The larger organism will not function. They had to develop compatible systems of communication so that their individual capabilities could contribute to the functioning of the whole.
Now about the purpose: the overall thriving of the ENTIRE organism, which required the consequences of individual cellular activities to become compatible with the needs of the larger body. Not just how an action affected the individual cell, or neighboring cells, or other cells of the same type, but how those activities contributed to the functioning of the organism as a whole.
And this is where resource allocation becomes particularly interesting to me.
When cells existed independently, they acquired resources from their environments and competed with other organisms for what they needed. Within a multicellular organism, resources could be distributed through shared systems, with different cells receiving different amounts according to their functions and circumstances.
Our bodies demonstrate this beautifully. The brain, muscles, skin, and digestive system all have different requirements, and those requirements change constantly. Blood circulation, chemical signals, and feedback mechanisms help direct resources where they’re needed. When we exercise, our muscles require more oxygen. When we eat, our digestive system becomes more active. When we’re injured, the body coordinates an immune response and begins repairing damaged tissue.
The cells aren’t receiving identical allocations, and there isn’t a central administrator calculating what each one deserves. Their relationships have developed mechanisms that allow resources to circulate and responses to adjust as conditions change.
Of course, these systems aren’t perfect. Cells can become cancerous, communication can fail, and the organism can develop diseases that interfere with resource distribution. But even those failures may help us understand what makes integration sustainable and what causes it to break down.
The same applies to communication. Cells use chemical and electrical signals, receptors, and feedback systems to coordinate their activities. A signal only becomes useful when another cell can recognize it and respond appropriately. Over evolutionary time, increasingly sophisticated systems of communication and regulation developed alongside cellular specialization.
And there is another remarkable development: individual cells can sometimes cease functioning or undergo programmed cell death as part of the organism’s normal development and maintenance. The continued existence of every individual cell is no longer necessary for the continued existence of the organism.
What interests me is how these relationships developed. How did previously independent organisms become sufficiently compatible that their individual activities could sustain a larger living system? How did communication, resource sharing, specialization, and regulation evolve together? And what changed in the consequences of their individual actions once they became part of a larger whole?
These are questions evolutionary biology has already investigated in considerable depth, and I think it would be interesting to overlay those findings on this theory to understand what that research tells us about how competition actually became cooperation, allocation and calibration. Understanding how that happened sustainably may be one of the most important pieces of understanding the larger trajectory of life and what’s next for us.
Part 3: Historical Significance
And I don’t think we are the first to ask these questions or explore the possibility of eventual connectedness. When I look at the world's enduring spiritual traditions through this lens, I can't help wondering whether many of their teachings about how to live, how to treat one another, and how to exist in harmony with the world were actually laying the groundwork for this kind of integration? Perhaps they were recognizing something fundamental about the natural order long before we had the scientific language and tools to explain why those relationships matter. What if we've been carrying pieces of these instructions for thousands of years without fully understanding what they might make possible?
Across the world's major spiritual traditions, we find recurring ideas of an underlying order: Logos in Christianity and Greek philosophy, Dharma in Hinduism and Buddhism, Dao in Daoism, and divine order in Judaism and Islam—different understandings of a universe governed by principles rather than randomness alone.
These traditions also recognize Consequence: karma in Hinduism and Buddhism, sowing and reaping in Christianity, moral accountability in Judaism and Islam, and the consequences of acting with or against the Dao.
And perhaps most fascinatingly, they repeatedly return to connectedness: dependent origination in Buddhism, the unity of existence in Hindu philosophy, the interconnectedness of creation in Abrahamic traditions, and harmony with nature in Daoism—all exploring, in profoundly different ways, how individual existence relates to something larger than itself.
These same themes are reflected in Zoroastrianism, Sikhism, Confucianism, Jainism, and many Indigenous spiritual traditions.
I'm starting to believe that Logos is not some mysterious force we need to invent to explain what science has already discovered, it’s the opposite. Logos and order could be the foothold ancient people used to describe something we can now explore the mechanisms of with science. We don’t question whether nature has mechanisms, because its obvious there are principles that govern the processes we see. But what if the principles that underlie the mechanisms are driven by an even deeper layer of coherent relational logic that organizes it all?
Part 4: Our Work
Looking back at our single celled ancestors coming together freely I wonder, if there were certain cells that understood the assignment and managed these transitions, helped the groups of cells replace their previous desires for competition with a singular purpose that could be expressed in their different tasks. 
What if as humans we can understand the natural order well enough to not only participate in it more constructively, but to apply that understanding to create new outcomes. Manage this next transition, as Adam was tasked with in the Garden of Eden.
If we explore this honestly and with an open mind, might we discover that some of our biggest problems aren't fundamentally separate problems at all? What if they are non ideal consequences of relationships that aren't functioning constructively under Logos or natural order?
Instead of becoming endlessly better at managing the symptoms, perhaps we could understand enough about the underlying relationships to make some of those problems less likely to arise. 
And I have a feeling this starts with a mathematical expression of the integrated consequences or reactions to all the actions that are taken. 
I know this is a very big idea.
I also know that I'm coming to it without the academic training that would allow me to recognize all the places where these questions have already been asked, answered, or disproven. I don't yet have the language or tools to investigate it properly. I would love your perspective, particularly on the human side of this question.
How have societies developed the shared meanings, obligations, rituals, and relationships that allow people to cooperate beyond immediate kinship or individual advantage?
What does anthropology tell us about how collective identity develops, and where cooperation breaks down? And where might these be reflected in biological processes?
I suspect the most useful next step is to bring together people who understand different pieces of this: anthropology, evolutionary biology, symbiosis, complex systems, and perhaps comparative religion or philosophy. People who also have the ability to question deeply, think outside the box and a desire to work on something new that draws on our collective knowledge but isn’t derivative.
I'm not asking anyone to endorse a theory of everything. I'm asking whether there's a worthwhile research question here, what existing scholarship I should be reading, and whether anyone might be interested in helping me sharpen it.
Because if life has repeatedly developed new capacities through the integration of previously independent parts, what can those transitions teach us about the conditions that might make another transition possible? And what would our role in it be? This might be the most important question no one is asking…

